# Documentation

## Placement and ownership

- Inspect the existing documentation structure before choosing a location.
  Extend the canonical document rather than adding a competing explanation.
- Keep READMEs as entry points that orient readers and link to detailed guides.
  Keep narrowly scoped operational notes near the code they explain when that
  matches the project's established structure.
- Add new documents to the existing index or relevant entry point so readers
  can discover them. Update an existing feature registry when the change
  affects what it inventories.

## Content and links

- Use relative links for documentation within the repository.
- Reference actual paths and symbols instead of copying implementations likely
  to change. Use the project's established domain terminology.
- Distinguish confirmed behavior from proposals and unverified runtime claims.
- Use placeholders in examples that would otherwise reveal credentials,
  connection strings, or private customer data.

## Maintenance

When behavior changes, update affected documentation in the same change.
Before finishing, verify that local links resolve and instructions match the
actual project. Remove obsolete statements instead of leaving contradictory
versions for readers to reconcile.
